# Payment Bot

Telegram bot that sells time-limited access to a private channel. Users pick a tariff, pay via LiqPay, and receive a one-time invite link. The bot reminds them before the subscription ends and removes them from the channel when it expires.

## Features

- Tariffs: 1 month, 3 months, lifetime
- LiqPay checkout link generated per order
- Payment callback with signature verification, amount/currency check and idempotent handling
- Subscriptions: activation, extension of an active subscription, expiry
- One-time invite links (24h, single use) and `/access` to re-issue a link
- Scheduler: reminder 3 days before expiry, automatic removal from the channel
- Admin commands (`/stats`, `/payments`, `/subscribers`), access controlled by membership in a Telegram admin group
- Docker Compose setup, migrations applied on startup

## Tech stack

Python 3.13, aiogram 3, FastAPI + Uvicorn, SQLAlchemy 2.0 (async) + asyncpg, PostgreSQL 16, Alembic, APScheduler 3, pydantic-settings, Docker Compose.

## How a payment flows

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant B as Bot (aiogram)
    participant DB as PostgreSQL
    participant L as LiqPay
    participant A as API (FastAPI)

    U->>B: /start, choose tariff
    B->>DB: create order (pending)
    B->>U: "Pay" button with signed LiqPay link
    U->>L: pays
    L->>A: POST /liqpay/callback (data, signature)
    A->>A: verify signature, amount, currency
    A->>DB: order=paid, activate/extend subscription (one transaction)
    A->>U: confirmation + invite link
```

## Project structure

```
app/
├── api/          # FastAPI: LiqPay callback, DB session dependency
├── bot/          # handlers, keyboards, middleware, admin filter
├── db/           # models, session, repositories, seed
├── services/     # liqpay, subscriptions, access, stats, admin
├── scheduler.py  # reminders and expiry jobs
├── config.py     # settings from environment
└── main.py       # runs the bot, the API and the scheduler in one process
alembic/          # migrations
scripts/          # helper scripts (callback simulation, channel checks)
```

## Quick start (Docker)

1. Create a bot with @BotFather, a private channel (add the bot as admin with the rights to invite and ban users) and an admin group (add the bot).
2. Copy the config and fill in the values:
   ```bash
   cp .env.example .env
   ```
3. Run:
   ```bash
   docker compose up -d --build
   docker compose logs -f app
   ```

`PUBLIC_URL` must be a public HTTPS address that LiqPay can reach (use a tunnel such as ngrok or Cloudflare Tunnel during development, a real domain in production).

## Local development

```bash
docker compose up -d db
python -m venv venv && source venv/bin/activate   # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
alembic upgrade head
python -m app.db.seed
python -m app.main
```

Run everything from the project root, using `python -m`.

## Configuration

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `BOT_TOKEN` | Telegram bot token |
| `CHANNEL_ID` | ID of the private channel with the content |
| `ADMIN_GROUP_ID` | ID of the admin group; its members can use admin commands |
| `POSTGRES_*` | Database credentials, host and port |
| `LIQPAY_PUBLIC_KEY`, `LIQPAY_PRIVATE_KEY` | LiqPay keys (sandbox keys for testing) |
| `PUBLIC_URL` | Public HTTPS base URL used for the LiqPay callback |

## Commands

| Command | Who | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `/start` | everyone | show tariffs, create an order |
| `/access` | active subscribers | get a new invite link |
| `/stats`, `/payments`, `/subscribers` | admins | revenue, last payments, active subscribers |

## Testing the payment flow without LiqPay

`scripts/fake_callback.py` sends a correctly signed callback to the local API:

```bash
python -m scripts.fake_callback <order_uuid> <amount>
```

It is used to verify the signature check, the amount check, idempotency (a repeated callback changes nothing) and subscription extension.

## Status and limitations

- The callback logic is verified with simulated callbacks. A real LiqPay sandbox payment has not been run end-to-end yet, so the exact callback fields and statuses (`success` / `sandbox`) should be confirmed against the first real callback.
- The scheduler runs inside the application process, so run a single instance (several instances would duplicate jobs).
- The bot uses long polling.
- No automated tests yet.
- Channel owners and admins cannot be removed by a bot, so their subscriptions are only deactivated.